From d66e7fedf8ce39a4f073c457e9187962f54ece17 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 17:25:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(live): the live check asserted a phrase nothing emits and
 sent operators to a runbook that does not exist
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The first proper post-deploy run turned up two failures, and neither was a fault
in the site.

1. verify-live.sh asserted that /llms-full.txt contains "complete technical
   corpus". The generator says "complete published corpus" and has for some
   time, so that line could only ever fail — reporting 265 KB of correct corpus
   as a broken deploy. That is precisely the failure mode verify-live.sh's own
   header warns about: the moment a check cries wolf, the next real failure gets
   waved through as "probably that flaky one again".

2. Five places across four files told the operator to follow
   old-domain/EXECUTE.md. That file did not exist. Neither did
   docs/outreach/DOMAIN_CONSOLIDATION.md, CLAIMS_REGISTER.md or
   WHY_NO_AGGREGATE_RATING.md, all named by guards as the reasoning behind what
   they enforce. An instruction that names a missing file does not read as an
   error; it reads as a task somebody else already documented — which is the
   likeliest reason the domain consolidation has never been executed.

That matters because the same run measured the old domain: ecowoodshardwood.com
answers HTTP 200 on 35 URLs, a second live site competing with ecowoods.ca for
the same entity, holding 22 customer testimonials with real names. The redirect
map, the four generated configs and the host-conditioned rules in vercel.json
were all already correct and already deployed. The only missing piece was the
page telling somebody which of them to execute and in what order.

All four runbooks are now written. old-domain/EXECUTE.md gives both routes —
attaching the domain to this Vercel project, where the rules already ship, and
uploading .htaccess to the Apache host that serves it today — with the
verification that must report 36 correct before any change of address is filed,
because telling Google a move happened while the old site still answers 200 is
worse than saying nothing.

scripts/verify-live-contract.mjs makes both classes checkable offline: every
md_check want string must appear literally in the generator that produces that
document, and every old-domain/ or docs/ path a script instructs an operator to
read must exist. It is scoped to instruction lines, so state-of-truth.sh may go
on asserting that old-domain/vercel.json is absent. Regression-tested against an
unsatisfiable assertion, a deleted runbook, and a renamed md_check that would
have left the guard silently blind.

Also inverts the "known-dead paths (must stay 404)" block. It asserted that
apps/web/public is unserved, which was true and worth documenting until FIX-01.
It now asserts those files serve, so a silent regression in the static-root sync
fails the live check instead of printing a warning nobody reads.

